E=[('Redline 2 · the two held paragraphs', 51, 'And this is no medieval invention. Centuries before St. Thomas systematized them — and before a word of the New Testament was written — Scripture had already named the cardinal virtues, prudence among them. The Book of Wisdom says of God\'s own wisdom that *"she teaches self-control and prudence, justice and courage; nothing in life is more profitable for men than these"* (Wisdom 8:7). There, in those very pages, the fourfold pattern of virtue stands fully drawn, with prudence at its head. What we are unfolding is not a later accretion onto the faith; it is read straight out of Scripture.', 'And this is no medieval invention. Long before St. Thomas set them in order, Scripture had already named the four virtues the Church calls *cardinal*, from the Latin word for "hinge," because "all the others are grouped around them" (*CCC* 1805). The verse from the Book of Wisdom that opens this chapter names all four: self-control, prudence, justice and courage. What we are unfolding is not something added to the faith later; it is read out of Scripture.'), ('Redline 2 · the two held paragraphs', 154, "If it were *your* uncontrollable cry dooming the cellar, you could offer your own life as a voluntary sacrifice — or you could risk yourself as a distraction, drawing the soldiers' attention in hopes of saving the others and the baby.", 'If it were *your* uncontrollable cough dooming the cellar, you could climb the stairs and give yourself up to the soldiers — or you could risk yourself as a distraction, drawing their attention in hopes of saving the others and the baby. Love may lay down its own life for another; it may never take an innocent life, its own included.')]
